nt doesn't continue  entirely as normal there's this thing  called perder persian for cloak whoever  said british politics was off-putting  huh it's basically just a set of rules  which means that a government cannot do  anything which might unduly influence  the election or take major decisions  which might bind their success and so  for example taking major spending  decisions  then you have the campaign that's a five  six or seven week period for how do you  win Britain is divided up into six  hundred and fifty constituencies each  constituency roughly with seventy  thousand voters each returns one MP to  the House of Commons they're elected by  a system called first-past-the-post  nothing to do with horse racing every  voter gets one vote only and the  candidate who receives more votes than  anyone else wins doesn't matter they  don't have a majority of the vote even  if it's just one more vote than the next  person  they are the winner the aim of the  election is to get a majority more MPs  for your party than all of the other  parties put together at a magic number  for this House of Commons is 326 if you  do that you are automatically the  government and your leader becomes Prime  Minister if you don't you come second  then you become the opposition and your  leader becomes leader of the Opposition  historically that has been the beauty of  the system its simplicity one side wins  one side loses one side governs one  opposes until they all play again but  recently it's got a bit more complicated  because well mystical parties and  politicians are starting to become very  bad at winning elections if no party  wins a majority then we have a composite  then parties must try and form a  coalition as happened in 2010 with David  Cameron and Nick Clegg Dems daddy why  are you spending so much time with this  man Nick leg or something short of full  coalition were a smaller party  guarantees a government state in office  in exchange for certain policy  commitments o